# core:signal -- clean shutdown on SIGTERM/SIGINT, and nothing else. A thin,
# turnkey FFI over sigaction(2) and shutdown(2); the companion signal_shim.c is
# pure libc, so there is nothing external to install and nothing to link (the same
# self-contained model as core:os and core:net). Import with `import "core:signal"`.
#
#   signal.on_shutdown(fd) -> bool
#                             Install a SIGTERM and SIGINT handler whose ONLY
#                             action is shutdown(fd, SHUT_RDWR) on the listening
#                             socket `fd`. Linux wakes blocking net.accept calls;
#                             portable servers use net.accept_wait so they also
#                             check the shutdown flag on a bounded timer. Returns false
#                             if the handler could not be installed -- fail closed,
#                             the default disposition (die on SIGTERM) stays.
#   signal.shutdown_requested() -> bool
#                             Has such a signal arrived? Reads the flag the
#                             handler set. For a loop that wants to stop for a
#                             reason other than a failed accept.
#   signal.register_conn(slot, fd) -> bool
#                             Publish an ACCEPTED connection fd so the handler
#                             shuts that down too. `slot` is the caller's worker
#                             id, 0..255. false if the slot or fd is out of range
#                             -- fail closed, and the connection then behaves
#                             exactly as it did before: released by its own
#                             SO_RCVTIMEO rather than by the signal.
#   signal.retire_conn(slot)  Clear a slot. CALL IT BEFORE close(fd), never
#                             after: a slot still holding a closed fd names a
#                             number the kernel may have handed back out.
#
# WHY THE CONNECTION HALF EXISTS. Shutting down the listener releases accept(2)
# and nothing else. A worker already blocked in recv(2) on an accepted connection
# waits out its full SO_RCVTIMEO -- measured on tycho-httpd at 4878 ms with
# --idle-ms 5000 and four idle keep-alive clients, all of it one idle timeout,
# against 1 ms with the registry in place (5 runs of 5).
# The registry is a fixed 256-slot array of `volatile sig_atomic_t`, one slot per
# worker, so registering and retiring need no lock: each slot has exactly one
# writer and the handler is a reader. A mutex is not async-signal-safe and taking
# one in handler context deadlocks against the interrupted thread; the design
# avoids needing mutual exclusion rather than trying to make it safe. The full
# argument, statement by statement, is in signal_shim.c.
#
# THE WIDTH IS THE DESIGN. There is deliberately no `signal.on(sig, handler)`.
# Calling a Tycho function from handler context is a language feature, not a
# library one, and it would need answers this package does not have: what a
# handler may allocate (the arena allocator is not re-entrant and every Tycho
# value lives in it), what it may do to a task (the scheduler's queues are
# mutex-guarded, and a handler that interrupts the lock holder deadlocks the
# process), and what happens when the signal lands on an arbitrary worker thread
# rather than main -- process-directed signals go to whichever thread the kernel
# picks. Adding it later means: a signal-safe way to hand the signal number to
# ordinary code (a self-pipe or signalfd read by a dedicated thread), a rule that
# the handler itself still touches nothing but a sig_atomic_t, and a spec section
# for the re-entrancy contract. None of that is needed to shut a server down, and
# the tree has exactly one caller.
#
# So this package runs NO Tycho code in handler context. The handler is three
# statements of C -- a store to a sig_atomic_t, a load from one, and shutdown(),
# each on the POSIX async-signal-safe list; see the header of signal_shim.c for
# the per-call argument.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS. `shutdown` waking a blocked accept() is Linux behaviour, not a
# POSIX guarantee. The server therefore uses net.accept_wait with a 100ms ceiling;
# Linux still gets the immediate wakeup, while macOS winds down on that timer.
# The bounded wait costs at most ten idle wakeups per worker each second.
# Connections still in the backlog are dropped. close(fd) remains unsafe: it
# released only 1/4 Linux accept loops and let the fd number be reused while three
# threads still referenced it. The table is in plan.md's phase 1 evidence.
#
# ORDER MATTERS: call on_shutdown AFTER net.listen, with the listening fd, and
# BEFORE the accept loops start. The fd is registered before the handler is, so a
# signal arriving mid-install can never act on a descriptor that is not there yet.
package signal

# ---- shim externs (libc sigaction/shutdown; defined in signal_shim.c) ----
# The `sigx_` prefix keeps these off the real libc names -- `signal` and
# `sigaction` are libc symbols -- so each binds to the shim, not to the kernel
# wrapper (the same reason core:net uses `netx_`).
extern fn sigx_on_shutdown(fd: int) -> int
extern fn sigx_requested() -> int
extern fn sigx_conn_register(slot: int, fd: int) -> int
extern fn sigx_conn_retire(slot: int)

# Arm SIGTERM/SIGINT to shut down the listening socket `fd`. false if the handler
# could not be installed.
fn on_shutdown(fd: int) -> bool:
    return sigx_on_shutdown(fd) == 1

# Has SIGTERM or SIGINT been received since on_shutdown?
fn shutdown_requested() -> bool:
    return sigx_requested() == 1

# Publish an accepted connection fd in `slot` (the caller's worker id, 0..255) so
# the handler shuts it down as well as the listener. false = refused, and that
# connection keeps its pre-registry behaviour.
fn register_conn(slot: int, fd: int) -> bool:
    return sigx_conn_register(slot, fd) == 1

# Clear `slot`. BEFORE close(fd), never after -- see the header.
fn retire_conn(slot: int):
    sigx_conn_retire(slot)
